What is CTE (Common Table Expression) in SQL?
A CTE (Common Table Expression) is a temporary result set in SQL that you can reference within a SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, or DELETE query.
It makes complex queries more readable and can be used for recursive queries, filtering, or breaking down logic.

🔹 Syntax:
sql
Copy
Edit
WITH cte_name AS (
    SELECT ...
)
SELECT * FROM cte_name;
🔹 Example:
sql
Copy
Edit
WITH TopEmployees AS (
    SELECT name, salary
    FROM employees
    WHERE salary > 100000
)
SELECT * FROM TopEmployees;
This creates a virtual table TopEmployees that contains employees with salary > 100K, and then selects from it.

🔹 Uses of CTEs:
Improve query readability (instead of subqueries)

Reuse logic within a single SQL

Recursive operations (e.g., hierarchy/organization trees)

🔁 Recursive CTE Example (Bonus):
sql
Copy
Edit
WITH RECURSIVE EmployeeHierarchy AS (
    SELECT id, name, manager_id
    FROM employees
    WHERE manager_id IS NULL

    UNION ALL

    SELECT e.id, e.name, e.manager_id
    FROM employees e
    INNER JOIN EmployeeHierarchy eh ON e.manager_id = eh.id
)
SELECT * FROM EmployeeHierarchy;
This builds a full employee hierarchy using a self-join recursively.

